    "body": "# Add dynamic mode to type projector\n\nAdds a **Dynamic** mode to the type projector, alongside Expected and Self. It infers the type of the values that actually flowed through an expression at runtime, and colours the parts of that type runtime supplied rather than statics.\n\n<img width=\"962\" height=\"556\" alt=\"Screenshot From 2025-11-19 13-11-50\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/1768e30e-15a4-4edb-99e1-e6a702f10ed2\" />\n\n## How it works\n\n**Inferring the type.** `DynamicTypInfer` runs statics over each of a probe's sample values in the program's context and meets the results. It returns an `option`, so a set of samples with inconsistent types is distinguishable from success.\n\n**Deciding what turns green.** Only the components runtime supplied go green, not the whole type. Given `(fun x -> ^^statics((1, x)))(true)`, statics knows `(Int, ?)` and runtime says `(Int, Bool)`, so `Bool` is green and `Int` stays the ordinary type colour. `Typ.diff` decides this by walking the two types together and returning the ids in the dynamic one that the static one does not account for.\n\nTwo things make that harder than it sounds:\n\n- *The comparison has to run on the types as they will be laid out, not as they arrive.* Rendering first normalizes a type \u2014 desugaring `Sig`, inserting defensive parens as real nodes, padding out id slots \u2014 and those added nodes carry tokens of their own. Diffing the types as the caller passed them cannot name their ids, which is why parentheses inside a wholly runtime-derived type used to render in the static colour. Both types now go through `ExpToSegment.normalize_typ` first. It is not idempotent, so the normalized type is rendered once and handed back together with the ids to colour, rather than re-rendered later.\n- *Statics builds types with `Typ.temp`,* which stamps every node with the same `Id.invalid` sentinel. Left in, the whole set of ids collapses onto whichever tile the renderer emits first \u2014 which on a partially-known type means turning a component statics supplied green instead. The sentinels are replaced at the point where a type's ids become the ids of rendered tokens.\n\n`Typ.diff` also expands type aliases through `~ctx` to decide whether they differ, but returns the alias token's own id rather than the expansion's, since an alias renders as a single token. It terminates on cyclic alias chains. Sums get fine-grained diffing via `ConstructorMap.venn_regions`, falling back to colouring the whole dynamic Sum when the static type has constructors it lacks.\n\n**What you see.** The tiles named by that id set get a `dynamic` class, threaded through `ProjectorView` and `Code.re` as a `~classes` callback, and `proj-type.css` renders those green.\n\n**Probes under nested ascriptions.** In `3 : Int : Int` the two ascriptions are consistent, so the transition meets them and rewrites to a single `3 : Int` \u2014 and the inner `3 : Int` node is gone. A probe sitting on that node goes with it. That rewrite *is* the node's evaluation step, so there is no other point at which the probe could have observed its value, and on dev the ascription transition emits no side effects (`Transition.re`: `side_effects: []`), so nothing is recorded and the probe reports nothing. That matters for a feature whose only input is probe samples.\n\nAscription transitions now collect the value through a writer monad in `Ascriptions.re` and report it as a `RecordAscriptionProbe` side effect, so `^^probe(3 : Int) : Int` records `3`. Pattern matching deliberately opts out with `Sample.no_targets`: the scrutinee is already final there, so its probes fired at the stack evaluation actually passed through, and collecting again would double-count.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `Test_DynamicTypInfer.re` \u2014 inference from probe samples (basic types, meet behaviour, inconsistent samples, user-defined ADTs, unevaluated branches), and colouring driven through the same composition the projector runs.\n- `Test_DynamicTypIds.re` \u2014 properties tying the green ids to the render: every dynamic id is actually emitted, a wholly runtime-derived type has every tile green, a type runtime merely confirmed has none, and a normalized type already carries every id its rendering consumes.\n- `Test_DynamicTyp.re` \u2014 end-to-end dynamic type rendering.\n- `Test_Typ.re` \u2014 `Typ.diff` unit cases and the identity property.\n\n## Notes\n\nThe projector is not offered on type positions: `Info.typ` carries no expected or self type and types are not evaluated, so every mode would render `?`.\n",
